|| The TPK algorithm of Knuth and Trabb Pardo, "The Early Development of
|| Programming Languages" (Stanford STAN-CS-76-562, 1976), page 3, where it
|| is given in ALGOL 60: read eleven numbers a[0] to a[10]; then, from
|| a[10] down to a[0], print i and f(a[i]), where f(t) = Sqrt|t| + 5t^3,
|| or i and TOO LARGE if f(a[i]) exceeds 400. The paper does not cover CPL;
|| this is written as the documents of 1963 to 1966 write CPL.
||
|| The input, in tpk.in, is the paper's own example: 1 1.5 2 2.25 2.5 2.75
|| 3 3.25 3.5 4.5 5.5.
||
|| Changes from the ALGOL, besides the character set:
|| - The array is made by a call of Newarray (Elementary Programming
||   Manual 15) and named A, as the manual names the coefficient array of
||   its section 22, which also runs from 0 to 10.
|| - f is a let definition, its parameter real because no type is given
||   (Elementary Programming Manual 18; Main Features 17). abs is Mod and
||   sqrt is Sqrt, the names of the manual's list of library functions
||   (Elementary Programming Manual 4.4); the 1966 Reference Manual leaves
||   its basic functions to an Appendix 3 that was never finished.
|| - The descending cycle is step 10, -1, 0, as in Elementary Programming
||   Manual 22; the ascending one is 0 to 10 (13.3).
|| - y is defined at the head of the controlled block rather than declared
||   in the outer block, as the manual defines its locals (section 14).
|| - if ... else is a test-command (Elementary Programming Manual 10.4;
||   Reference Manual 9.6.3).
|| - Read and Write are the routines of the supplement for the pre-pilot
||   system. The 1966 Reference Manual has no input and output: its
||   section 1.4 was "postponed". The supplement's WRITE printed reals in
||   floating decimal to 7 figures; this system prints 12 significant
||   figures instead.

let A = Newarray[real, (0, 10)]
let f[t] = Sqrt[Mod[t]] + 5t**3

for i = 0 to 10 do Read[A[i]]
for i = step 10, -1, 0 do
   $( let y = f[A[i]]
      test y > 400
         then Write[i, ' TOO LARGE*n']
         or Write[i, ' ', y, '*n'] $)
